      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The common adverse effect of centrally-injected mu-opioid receptor (mu-OR) agonists is pruritus. This study was conducted using mice to examine whether different subtypes of mu-OR would be responsible for pruritus and analgesia. Intracisternal injections of morphine and morphine-6beta-glucronide (M6G), but not M3G, produced an antinociceptive effect. Morphine, but neither M6G nor M3G, induced facial scratching, a pruritus-related response. Facial scratching following morphine was not affected by the mu(1)-OR antagonist naloxonazine at doses that inhibited the antinociceptive effects. The results suggest that different subtype and/or splice variants of mu-OR are separately involved in pruritus and antinociception of opioids."},
